{
  "additional_concerns": [
    "The transition provision preserves an existing application process; it does not expressly establish permanent substantive ineligibility for every change with an application pending at commencement. Candidates node.1bf6918f69c6b37738daeb83, node.4e6a162c35678e9ddf17ad8e, node.ce439b1cf9ed8301fbb68f94 and node.ea4d19630f8cab09065b1909 encode that additional exclusion. Retain it as a proposed interpretation, not an established source rule.",
    "Node.4d548f2e09bc36ef51212f64 excludes legacy applications from scope rather than deciding their eligibility. Node.b66b3e831bb98a12db59dffa decides substantive conditions only. These differences must remain visible; neither resolves actual relief for a legacy application.",
    "The specified commencement date is supported, but the source does not identify a universal assessment or implementation event for every transitional situation. The circular's issuance date is also absent, so the relationship between immediate effect and the specified date remains unverified.",
    "Missing external materials for resolving actual transitional availability include the relevant SFC FAQs and any authoritative guidance on withdrawal, resubmission and subsequent application decisions. The supplied dependency IDs have no identifying descriptions or source hashes, so no reliable catalogue mapping is possible.",
    "Local policy implications, novelty, complexity and home compliance remain supplied judgments. Applying them to an actual change may require relevant SFC guidance and applicable home-jurisdiction law; these authorities are not supplied. This does not undermine the circular's express exception structure.",
    "No separate home-supervision prerequisite is expressly attached to item (ii). Importing item (i)'s condition or treating the introductory purpose as an additional executable prerequisite would require justification.",
    "The unavailable formalization for node.8eb374ffa546ddd2ba291e37 is an unresolved assessment, not an executable false result. None of the scoped correspondence findings establishes complete approval obligations, notification compliance or post-vetting compliance."
  ],
